gaps.proposedFix:
Add a variable-width length field kind plus a length-kind name usable inside `tlv`:
  { "berLen": "len(group:pdu)" }
  { "tlv": { "t":"u8", "l":"berLen", ... } }
Generalise it as a family so it is not SNMP-specific:
  { "varLen": { "encoding":"ber"|"leb128"|"quic"|"vlq", "of":"len(group:X)" } }
Engine amendment required alongside it (smallest possible): change §3's two-pass rule to "iterate the size-resolution pass to a fixpoint". Convergence is guaranteed because emitted size is monotone non-decreasing in the resolved values and bounded by `limits.maxBytes`. Cheaper alternative that also works and is arguably cleaner: resolve sizes bottom-up over the field tree BEFORE emitting, which is legal because span dependencies in a nesting grammar form a tree, never a cycle.

Make `group` a decode-mode construct with a bound, and give the decoder a region stack:
  { "group":"vbl", "limit":"capture.vblLen", "onOverrun":"error", "onUnderrun":"error", "fields":[ ... ] }
Expose two read-only expressions inside a region: `remaining` (bytes left in the innermost region) and `endOfRegion` (boolean). `tlv` with a `fields` body (GAP-8) opens a region implicitly, so the common case needs no extra syntax. This single change also retires the CoAP and DNS entries in §5's known-gaps list.

Allow `tlv` to take a body instead of a value, and allow a named parse block to be re-entered:
  { "tlv": { "t":"u8", "l":"berLen", "expect":"0x30" }, "as":"pdu", "fields":[ ... ] }
  { "parseAs": "parse.varBind", "in": "capture.vbRaw", "as":"vb" }
Declare `parseAs` self-recursion legal with a mandatory `"maxDepth": n` (n <= 32), mirroring §4's `loop:{max:n}` discipline so the work bound stays computable. This one change makes the grammar cover the whole ASN.1/BER family — LDAP, X.509, Kerberos, SNMPv3 — not just SNMP.

Let any capturing field retain its exact source span alongside the decoded value:
  { "tlv":{...}, "as":"requestId", "conv":"unDerInt", "keepRaw":"requestIdRaw" }
and let an encode field prefer a raw span when present:
  { "bytes":{ "of":"capture.requestIdRaw ?? (inputs.requestId | derInt)" } }
Then state the round-trip contract precisely in §7: decode(encode(x)) == x always; encode(decode(b)) == b only when raw spans are retained, or when the sender used DER-canonical form. Both halves of the bar are then honest and testable, instead of one being quietly false.

Delete `snmp` from the sugar table and add a reusable, protocol-independent one in its place:
  { "kind":"asn1ber", "tagging":"implicit", "canonical":"ber|der" }
which is nothing more than GAP-1 (berLen) + GAP-2 (oid) + GAP-3 (derInt/derUint) + GAP-8 (tlv with fields) packaged as a named preset over the `bytes` layer. SNMP, LDAP, X.509, Kerberos, SNMPv3 and PKCS all then fall out of one layer, and §7's no-bespoke-assembly rule is actually kept rather than quietly broken.
